CHEMICAL COMPOUNDS AND MOLECULES CREATABLE WITH H, O, AND Fe
===========================================================

This document outlines various chemical molecules, ions, and network compounds that can be constructed using only Hydrogen (H), Oxygen (O), and Iron (Fe).

1. MOLECULES CONTAINING ALL THREE ELEMENTS (Fe, O, H)
-----------------------------------------------------
* Fe(OH)2 (Iron(II) hydroxide)
  - Description: Composed of one iron atom bonded to two hydroxyl (OH) groups. Forms a pale green solid when iron reacts in basic water solutions.

* Fe(OH)3 (Iron(III) hydroxide)
  - Description: Composed of one iron atom bonded to three hydroxyl groups. A major component of rust, forming a reddish-brown precipitate.

* FeO(OH) (Iron(III) oxyhydroxide)
  - Description: Contains an oxide oxygen and a hydroxyl group. Naturally occurs as minerals like goethite or lepidocrocite, which are primary components of rust.

* Fe-OOH (Iron hydroperoxide intermediate)
  - Description: A highly reactive molecular fragment or transition state often found in biochemistry and catalysis, such as within the active sites of iron-containing enzymes or during Fenton's reagent chemical reactions.


2. REACTIVE RADICALS AND TRANSIENT COMPLEXES
--------------------------------------------
* FeO2+ (Ferryl ion / Oxoiron(IV) species)
  - Description: A powerful, highly reactive intermediate where iron is doubly bonded to oxygen (Fe=O).

* FeO2^2+ and FeO3^+ (Iron-peroxo and iron-superoxo complexes)
  - Description: Transient complexes that form in solution when iron reacts with hydrogen peroxide.


3. DUAL-ELEMENT COMPOUNDS (BINARIES)
------------------------------------
A. Iron & Oxygen (Fe + O)
* FeO (Iron(II) oxide)
  - Description: Known as wüstite, a black-colored mineral.

* Fe2O3 (Iron(III) oxide)
  - Description: Known as hematite, the primary ingredient in red rust.

* Fe3O4 (Iron(II,III) oxide)
  - Description: Known as magnetite, a naturally magnetic black mineral containing both oxidation states of iron.

B. Hydrogen & Oxygen (H + O)
* H2O (Water)
  - Description: Consists of two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom.

* H2O2 (Hydrogen peroxide)
  - Description: A highly reactive molecule with an extra oxygen atom, commonly used as a cleaning oxidant.

* HO* (Hydroxyl radical)
  - Description: A highly reactive, short-lived neutral single-oxygen-single-hydrogen radical.

C. Iron & Hydrogen (Fe + H)
* FeH (Iron hydride)
  - Description: A simple diatomic molecule. It is highly unstable under normal conditions but can be found in small amounts in extreme environments like stellar atmospheres.

